<!-- About: visible, plain-language summary for people, search and answer engines -->
<section class="section container--wide reveal" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="about-title">About</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="about">
    <div class="about__body">
      <p>
        I'm Shrinath Prabhu, a Senior Staff Frontend Engineer based in
        Mumbai, India, with over 8 years of professional experience. I work
        at Avail Project, where I lead frontend architecture across the Nexus
        product ecosystem.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Avail I conceived and shipped Avail Widgets (formerly Nexus One and
        Nexus Elements): React components for cross-chain swap, send, and
        deposit, distributed as the npm package
        <code>@avail-project/widgets</code> and a shadcn registry, and used
        by more than 10 dApps. I built the Widget Configurator, where
        developers explore settings and get the exact code to use, added fiat
        onramping via Meld to the Deposit Widget, and built Nexus Fastbridge,
        now live on 15 EVM chains with about $100,000 in monthly swap and
        bridge volume.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that I led frontend engineering at Arcana Network for 4.5
        years, from seed stage through its acquisition by Avail in 2025. I
        have shipped 4 Chrome Extensions to the Chrome Web Store with a 100%
        approval rate, including a non-custodial multi-chain wallet
        supporting 10+ blockchains (4.7 star rating, 750+ users).
      </p>
      <p>
        My highest-traction product, SendIt, grew to over 65,000 registered
        accounts and over 20,000 peak daily active users. The traffic
        exhausted Netlify Pro plan limits, so I migrated the frontend to
        self-managed AWS infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Earlier, at Hashbinary Services, I built Pricinger (a privacy Chrome
        Extension with about 1,000 users) and delivered a farmer learning
        platform MVP for Agrostar within a week.
      </p>
    </div>
